3. `src/hivemind/views/jev_matching.py` and `src/hivemind/jev.py`, against `src/hivemind/views/matching.py` (`build_lists`, `check`), `src/hivemind/views/writing.py` and `src/hivemind/views/round.py`: is the Jev usage sound and is it the best use of the API? Check in particular: the relation question asked only against `lists.candidate_lists` while `check` also accepts targets among the shown views; the succession rule (same key except a later period; it lost the one succession the model made across different scopes); membership added by appending stored group ids to `group_refs` (read `src/hivemind/views/groups.py` `plan` to confirm stored ids are accepted and how `basis` is recorded); evidence questions limited to the top-10 word-overlap list (`matching.rank_evidence`) when Jev could read every piece in force; one call per incoming piece versus one call per (item, thesis) with a `choice` over stored piece ids plus `none` (the vendor's "semantic_find" recipe at https://docs.typesafe.ai/cookbooks/semantic_find — do not fetch it, reason from the API shape: `choice` 1–255 options, first-option bias documented for jev-1.13); the pre-set 0.5 thresholds and whether an abstain band routed to the generative call (the old repository's cascade) would have caught the two errors on the seed (nvda-20260125 e5→v5 `supersedes` 0.77; q1fy27pr e4→v6 `same_statement` 0.70) or the earlier misses at 0.45–0.51; dependencies not asked (2 in the control week) and whether a Jev question could produce them (a `noul` per shown thesis plus a direction `choice`); `said_day` taken from the piece's own `original_day`; the second pass for evidence linked to merged candidates; archiving and cache semantics in `jev.py` (a byte-identical request served from the archive: when is that wrong?); failure handling (HTTP 429/529 retry, other errors fail the item).
