2316:## D165 · 2026-10-25 · Research groups enter the default pipeline; a group is an ongoing matter, a company's own financial lines carry none
2318:- Alex, on learning that the grouping research (D142–D150, D156–D162) had not reached the pipeline: "把我要做的grouping逻辑充分地scope in 做第一周管线的验证". Claude put the D149 contract in D147's final form into the default pipeline (`thesis_groups`, `thesis_group_members`, extraction v7 with `group_refs` and `new_groups`, the writer resolving local ids and storing memberships with the theses, matching deciding none), config `poc-v19`.
2319:- The first grouped seed day showed the company-folder failure (eight NVDA financial theses in one "AI infrastructure demand" group) because every candidate had to cite a group. Alex chose the correction from the two offered ("看起来可以你深度思考计划之后改再跑"): a research group is one ongoing matter in the world that several questions track together (a product rollout and its deployments, a financing or acquisition deal, a policy process, an investment or demand cycle), usually across companies and themes; a company's own reported or guided financial lines (total or segment revenue, margins, expenses, tax rate, capex) belong to the company and carry no group unless the line itself tracks such a matter. Code guards it: a declared group is created only when a written thesis that tracks a matter cites it; the lines may join a group another thesis founded. This amends D149's "every proposed thesis carries structured membership" and 06-views' "every candidate needs nonempty references". Config `poc-v20` (`role-v8`).
2323:## D166 · 2026-10-25 · Group identity and membership by names, in code
3:> Status: foundation frozen, October 20, 2026. D149 adds extraction-owned grouping; D165 puts it in the default pipeline (poc-v20) for validation on the first week. English edition: October 25, 2026 (D163). Only recorded decisions apply; open questions and two pull-mode proposals remain separate.
8:A thesis has an immutable question, ladder and due date. Evidence moves its state, which freezes when due. Search then compare candidates with existing theses to merge, continue, create or skip; compare evidence within its thesis to repeat, replace or add. Extra source references alone do not trigger rejudgement. Extraction determines group associations; the writer preserves them after resolving final thesis IDs under the [grouping contract](#research-groups-associate-during-extraction).
15:- The model judges from all evidence still in force and dependency conclusions, at low temperature, rather than adjusting its previous score. Replacement follows statement date, so late old statements do not restore obsolete evidence.
18:- Search finds plausible matches and the model compares their meaning. A match inherits the existing ladder and due date; a merely similar question is new. D155's current POC additionally gives code ownership of fully determined duplicate identity, with its limits in the improvement plan.
34:│       ├─ Link → Evidence (Store evidence once with sources and tier. Its push belongs to the link. Replaced evidence remains in history and no longer counts.)
36:│       └─ Succession → The previous period's thesis (Successive periods of the same focus form a chain)
37:├─ Research group → Organise theses by a continuing business process (Associate during extraction; full rules are in “Research groups: associate during extraction”)
38:└─ Append-only records → Matching, rejudgement, succession, closure, reopening and labelling records (Append at every step, without editing or deleting. Read records up to a historical date to recover its judgement.)
118:└─ Event thesis → Follow succession (Example: NVDA FY2027 China revenue → chip export licensing to China)
126:- Dependencies are the sole way theses influence each other; succession only orders periods. B depending on A means B reads A's conclusion. Keep B's direct evidence separate from dependency states and reasons. A's conclusion can affect B like evidence but is not stored as evidence: external events and statements are evidence, whereas A is our own judgement. Treating it as evidence would let the system convince itself.
133:## Succession: the same focus over successive periods
138:- Keep ladder orientation across succession: a strongly bullish first rung remains strongly bullish, although thresholds can change. Dependencies retain their direction; reversing the ends would break it.
147:| Decision | Existing identity or part, succession, new thesis or skip | Repeat, replace or new evidence |
148:| Chain | Succession: Fed year-end 2026 rate → 2027 → 2028 | Replacement: AMZN capex guidance 12% growth → 25% |
155:Process one round per item in sequence order. Candidates arrive with associations and necessary group definitions, preserved under the grouping rules. The model performs steps 4 and 7; code performs the others.
163:  ↓ 4 Match (Model): The model does not read the original item. Show each existing thesis's subject, question, focus, ladder, due date and open/closed state. In one call: (1) decide candidate identity or a narrower part, succession with unchanged ladder orientation but new thresholds, new thesis for distinct or merely similar questions, or skip overly broad questions, numbers beyond this/next year and the same period of a closed thesis; give reasons; (2) choose dependencies and directions among searched, block-5 and same-item new theses; (3) compare evidence linked only to existing theses. Evidence linked to candidates waits for their destinations, then searches all linked existing theses together and uses one further comparison call. Evidence linked only to new theses is new evidence.
165:  ↓ 6 Write to the library (Code): Resolve extraction's group associations to final theses and save them in this step. Each subject has its own writing queue; write theme theses before asset theses. Save new theses with their dependency edges. A repeat adds a source reference and any new thesis link. For replacement, order by when the statement was made: later statements supersede earlier ones. A late-arriving older statement is logged, not inserted. Carry the old evidence's other thesis links to its replacement. An original-source revision that omits a statement without replacing it marks it replaced. If compared data changes before writing, repeat matching only for the affected part.
175:| Merge into existing | The candidate concerns the same focus or a part of it, such as H20 China revenue within China revenue. Merge only into open theses; closed theses can only be succeeded. | Compare its evidence within the target thesis and preserve grouping under this section's rules. Use the existing ladder and due date. |
176:| Succession | The next period of the same focus: the next year for numbers, or continued tracking after an event thesis closed | Create a thesis and record succession |
180:- The D155 POC uses code for fully determined duplicate identity and model comparison when scope or legacy identity needs judgement. Reference lists guide research without vetoing candidates. Same group does not mean same thesis. See docs/poc/grouping-improvement-plan.md for the contract and measured scope.
181:- Search finds plausible candidates; the model handles semantic comparison. H20 and Blackwell export licences can look similar but are separate questions. Bullish and bearish accounts of one question share its identity.
182:- Search includes closed theses, allowing an FY2027 candidate to find closed FY2026 and be classified as succession.
190:| Repeat | The same evidence already exists under the thesis: a retelling, the same number in text and table, or the same speaker repeating the same statement | Add a source reference, not another evidence item. Existing links do not trigger rejudgement; a new thesis link changes that thesis's inputs and does. |
191:| Replace | A later account of the same matter: a speaker revises guidance, forecast or event progress; an official confirms/denies a rumour; or the original source revises its statement | Order by statement date. Later statements replace earlier ones, which remain in history; rejudge. A late-arriving older statement is only logged and does not trigger rejudgement. |
197:- Replacement applies to a statement or number, not the whole report. A revised sell-side revenue forecast replaces that forecast while other arguments remain in force.
223:| February 6 Reuters report retells the guidance | Repeat of g1 | Add a source reference to g1 | No rejudgement |
225:| April 30 call: guidance revised to 25% growth | Replace g1 | g2 is in force; g1 is replaced | Rejudge, reading “12% → 25%” |
226:| May 2 Morgan Stanley forecast revised to 27% | Replace m1 | m2 is in force; m1 is replaced | Rejudge |
228:| June 10 meeting reiterates “still around 25%” | Repeat of g2 | Add a source reference to g2, latest June 10 | No rejudgement |
254:| Received input | Preserve extraction's candidates, associations, new group definitions and evidence exactly. Separately record the matching-model input. |
288:| Research-group association | Group memberships, stored under this section's grouping rules | China licensing and business for this chip class |
289:| Succession | Predecessor, if any | Succeeds FY2026 |
306:| Replaced | The replacing evidence, where a speaker revises a statement/number, an official confirms/denies a rumour, or the original source revises/deletes it. Deletion has no replacement evidence. Replaced evidence no longer counts but remains in history. |
315:- One item's changes form a round. Finish evidence links, thesis creation, succession and dependencies, then rejudge in dependency order. Dependants read conclusions freshly judged in that round. Judge each thesis once. A widely depended-on thesis such as the Fed rate triggers each dependant once only if it declares its conclusion changed; otherwise propagation stops.
316:- Append-only records: questions, ladders and due dates are immutable. Matching, rejudgement, succession, closure, reopening and labels append records with sequence numbers.
319:- On failure, record the item's completed step and retry from the failed step, replacing only that attempt's own result. Repeated failures go to human review.
329:| Extraction | Receive sourced evidence, candidates, associations and necessary group definitions. Supply open subject theses and their dependencies: question, focus, due date and links, without states/evidence, plus available groups under this contract. |
338:- Preserve extraction's associations against final thesis IDs and trace real evidence links under the [grouping contract](#research-groups-associate-during-extraction). Default integration is pending; isolated code checks do not satisfy this acceptance.
343:- Classify every evidence comparison as repeat, replace or new. A repeat adds a reference without rejudging existing links; a new link to an open thesis triggers its rejudgement. Replaced evidence stays in history.
346:- Choose dependencies during matching from shown theses or explicitly awaited chain/years. Numeric dependencies read the specified year; event dependencies follow succession. No cycles.
375:- D38–D40: question/ladder/due-date structure, state, evidence links/tiers, dependencies/succession, round-based intake, history and matching labels.
377:- D44: initial model-owned identity and step-4 matching. D45: distinguish recorded decisions from proposals; similar questions remain separate. D155 later bounds code-owned identity in the POC.
387:- D149: this module owns group handoff/storage; matching does not regroup extraction's associations.
391:## Research groups: associate during extraction
394:- D149 requirement: organise theses by the continuing business process they discuss, called a research group. Vera Rubin readiness and customer deployment can share a group while shipment dates, SK factory launch and OpenAI deployment retain separate questions, ladders and due dates. A group may start with one thesis; a thesis may belong to several groups. A group has no state or ladder, creates no automatic dependency and is not an overarching investment thesis for an agent.
395:- Associate by business process, across companies and industries, not merely by company. A customer deployment explicitly using Rubin can join Rubin; NVIDIA PC chips cannot join just because they share a company. OpenAI deployment cannot join an SK-specific group merely because both involve Rubin. A group does not replace a research-universe theme.
396:- Before the full-text call, code supplies available group IDs, names and scopes. Search is not restricted by company. During that call, extraction references shown groups or declares new named and scoped groups. A candidate that tracks an ongoing matter (a product rollout and its deployments, a deal, a policy process, an investment or demand cycle) cites one or more groups, each shown or declared, without repeats; a company's own reported or guided financial lines (total or segment revenue, margins, expenses, tax rate, capex) belong to the company and cite none unless the line itself tracks such a matter (D165). Code validates the references before writing, and creates a declared group only when a written thesis that tracks a matter cites it, so a company's lines alone never found a group. Declare a new group if none fits; do not force an approximate fit.
397:- Matching still decides thesis/evidence identity and dependencies; it does not reread association fields to regroup. After creation, succession or merging, the writer resolves candidate IDs to final thesis IDs and local group IDs to stored group IDs, then stores membership. Candidates failing source checks or ultimately skipped do not become members. Do not create empty groups for unused declarations or reject other valid memberships because of them.
398:- Store each group/member pair once, with input ID and sequence number under the library's ordering and historical-read rules. Commit groups and memberships atomically with theses and evidence; roll all back on failure. Never mark a partial write complete. Redelivery must not duplicate membership; use existing dispatch and recovery.
399:- Groups do not copy evidence. Read sources through members' real evidence links, filtered by the requested sequence and excluding evidence already replaced at that point. No link means no evidence. A new member or an existing thesis change flag updates its groups. A repeated quote only adds a source and does not trigger an update. Evidence-only articles update existing memberships without inventing theses or groups.
400:- Acceptance measures source-supported membership and evidence retention, not identical names, IDs or group counts on every run. Report omissions, check refusals, missed associations and wrong associations separately. Preserve Rubin shipment/customer-deployment relationships and check wrong links to unrelated products or customer-specific groups. Multi-company and large-library behaviour is not yet validated.
402:- See docs/poc/grouping-improvement-plan.md for gaps and steps. D148 member context and D150 reason-first output did not solve wrong grouping. D152's four name-selection responses to the third article created no candidates, so new membership was unexercised. Preserve all prior responses and results. Overarching thesis generation, MCP presentation and historical backfill are outside that implementation scope.

